On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:07:50 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> So, research how they track versions of patch series, and how they
> indicate states like approved, rejected, etc. Report on what you find so
> that we can develop a workflow.

Here is what I found. Each project has two parts: how it links the
versions of a series, and where it keeps a series' state. Only one of
them keeps that state in the mail itself.

Linux kernel. Each version is a new thread, marked [PATCH vN]. The
cover letter ends with "Changes in vN" and a "Link to v(N-1)" line.
b4 writes both, and it puts a change-id in the cover letter that
stays the same across versions. Approval goes in reply trailers:
Acked-by, Reviewed-by and Tested-by. A Reviewed-by on the cover
letter covers the whole series. "b4 trailers" and "b4 am" collect
them and write them into the commit message. Nobody has defined a
rejection trailer. A NAK, or "Nacked-by", is written in prose, and a
maintainer says "applied, thanks" ("b4 ty") or "dropped" in prose too.
The real state lives outside the mail, in Patchwork
(patchwork.kernel.org). Its states are New, Under Review, Accepted,
Rejected, RFC, Not Applicable, Changes Requested, Awaiting Upstream,
Superseded and Deferred. Maintainers set them by hand, and a bot sets
some of them when commits land.

OpenBSD. Patches go to tech@ inline. A new version is usually posted
in the same thread ("updated diff below"), so there is nothing to
supersede. Approval is "ok kettenis@" in a reply, and the committer
copies it into the commit message ("ok kettenis@ claudio@"). There is
no tracker and no rejection marker. A diff that nobody oks just goes
nowhere. The only durable state is the commit log.

PostgreSQL. Patches are attachments named v2-0001-*.patch, and every
version stays in one thread. The state is in the CommitFest app
(commitfest.postgresql.org). Each entry links to its mail threads and
has one of these statuses: Needs Review, Waiting on Author, Ready for
Committer, Committed, Returned with Feedback, Rejected, Withdrawn, or
Moved to next CF. cfbot applies the newest patch set it finds in the
thread. The commit carries "Reviewed-by:" and a "Discussion:" trailer
that links to the thread by Message-ID. That links a commit back to
its thread, but nothing in the mail marks the thread as done.

FreeBSD also seems worth a look, given our commit style. Commit
messages carry "Reviewed by:", "Approved by:" (the mentor or re@
sign-off), and "Differential Revision:". The review state lives in
Phabricator.

Git (git.git) keeps its state in the list. Junio posts "What's cooking
in git.git" regularly, and it gives the state of every topic: "Will
merge to next", "Expecting a reroll", "Will discard", and so on.
Every state comes from one maintainer message.

sourcehut (lists.sr.ht) is the closest match to what you propose. A
reply in a patchset thread can carry the header
"X-Sourcehut-Patchset-Update:" with one of PROPOSED, NEEDS_REVISION,
SUPERSEDED, APPROVED, REJECTED or APPLIED. The list sets the
patchset's state from it.

So the big three keep series state in a web tool, and only use mail
for approval trailers. Sourcehut and Git keep it in mail. Your goal
of deriving state from the list puts us with them.

> Again a goal of this workflow is to have the current state of the list
> be derivable from the contents of the list. So I won't tag things as
> overcome or accepted - those need to be derived from the
> contents. Supporting trailers - or a similar mechanism - lets me add
> metadata to emails after the fact.

That fits what is already on the list. "[PATCH] notmuch: Tag patches
reported committed" [1] adds a header_ids helper to mail-tag.sh. It
pulls a Message-ID out of a named header, and the committed tag is
derived from it on every notmuch new. Your choice there of a header
naming the Message-ID [2] carries over to this. Here is a shape I
would propose, for you to decide on:

1. Key names. Use hyphens: Superseded-by, Approved-by, Rejected-by. I
   checked "git interpret-trailers --parse". It does not accept
   "Overcome by:" with a space, and one invalid line voids the whole
   trailer block. I would also say "superseded" rather than
   "overcome", since Patchwork and sourcehut both use it, but the name
   is yours to pick.

2. Header or body trailer. Body trailers are visible and easy to type
   in any client, and git interpret-trailers can parse them. They need
   care, though. Quoted lines must not count, and a signature after
   the trailers stops git from finding them. A header like the
   Committed: one is unambiguous, but it is hidden and harder to write
   by hand. I lean toward body trailers parsed with git
   interpret-trailers, applied only to the unquoted last paragraph.

3. Scope. A trailer applies to the message the reply's In-Reply-To
   names. A reply to the cover letter, or to a lone patch, applies to
   the whole thread. A reply to patch N/M applies only to that patch.
   This gives you both commit-level and thread-level approval and
   rejection with one rule. Tagging a thread needs a thread lookup.
   Today mail-tag.sh only tags single messages.

4. Authority. Anyone can write a trailer, including me, so the hook
   should count Approved-by and Rejected-by only when the From is a
   maintainer address. Superseded-by could also be accepted from the
   series' own author. If you want "the latest trailer wins" when two
   disagree, ordering by Date works.

5. Supersession without an extra reply. Every revised cover letter
   already ends with "Link to v(N-1): id:<msgid>", which
   src/lab-process-mail/prompt.md requires. The hook could derive
   superseded for the v(N-1) thread from that line. The explicit
   Superseded-by reply would then be for cases the Link line does not
   cover, such as a series replaced by a different one.

6. Hiding. Add superseded (and maybe rejected) to search.exclude_tags
   in the notmuch config. The threads are then still there when you
   search for them by tag.

Open questions for you: the key names, header or body, which
addresses count as maintainers, and whether the Link line should
supersede on its own. Once you decide, I will post a series that
builds on [1]. It would add the trailer parser and thread-level
tagging to mail-tag.sh, the exclude tags to the config, and a section
in doc/guides/patch-workflow.org.

